2026-09-27: benchmark result integrity; not a new musl runtime PASS.

musl minimal failures (tools/ladder/tests/test_musl.py): a fake make exits 7
while an old libc.a exists. The original real shell adapter exits zero and
prints tests-done. The original Python adapter also accepts a nonzero runner
with an old marker, a substring marker, missing runtime cases, omitted failures
in the aggregate report and a mismatched installed libc archive.

Repair: fail on infrastructure exit; require an exact terminal marker; rebuild
the generated archive; inventory original libc-test ERRS through make debug;
require every runtime result and keep all observed failures in REPORT. Verify
rebuilt/installed archive identity, preserved reference watchdog identity and
all selected EvoCC object hashes before/after execution. Record each trial's
hashes/logs. Reject incomplete substitution; replay the final passing set even
if bisection's answer came from a cache; restore reference objects in finally.
Original libc-test recipes already record ordinary test/build failures without
failing make. Removing the adapter's extra '|| true' therefore rejects broken
infrastructure without discarding expected shared FAIL cases.

Positive/negative fixture trials cover successful/failed final replay, missing
objects, invalid inventories, changed watchdog, object replacement and exact
runner exits. The small complete report retains its shared FAIL as FAIL.
These tests execute the failure-injected shell and public Python adapter,
including receipt construction, but do not run the real musl/library campaign.

cJSON regression: the full Python gate exposed an intermittent stale-library
failure. Equal restored-object/archive timestamps make this deterministic:
the previous bad utils archive can survive a good-object restore. The RED is
inside tools/ladder/tests/test_cjson.py, with controlled timestamps. Relinking
now removes only generated archives and executables, forcing consumption of
the independently audited objects. No input source/assertion changed.

